        "retrieval": 66.43,
        "fast_filter": 0.98,
        "validation": 2577.34,
        "generation": 36333.62,
        "total": 49016.06
      },
      "answer": "### Both Stage One and Stage Two of the field calibration procedure involve the following steps based on the provided handbook sections:\n\n#### **Stage One**\n**Procedure:** Before field deployment, technicians must perform Stage One zero-point alignment inside a sealed nitrogen reference chamber maintained at 20.0 degrees Celsius. They wait 18 minutes for thermal equilibrium before issuing the ZERO\\_LOCK command over the serial maintenance port.\n\n#### **Zero Point Alignment Steps:**\n1. Perform zero-point calibration inside a nitrogen-sealed chamber at 10\u00b0C.\n2. Allow time for thermal equilibration (approximately 15 minutes).\n3. Issue the `ZERO\\_LOCK` command via the serial port.\n\n---\n\n#### **Verification Step (Stage Two)**\n**Purpose:** After performing stage one, technicians verify the span of their sensors connected to a calibrated standard.\n\n**Procedure:**\n- Connect the manifold to a certified 113.25 hectopascal calibration standard.\n- Record the residual offset on diagnostic register 0x1A.\n- Verify the result against the threshold: \n  - If the absolute offset exceeds 1.2 hectopascals, replace the silicone intake gasket and retry the process.\n\n#### Verification Steps:\n1. Connect the sensor\u2019s manifold to the calibration standard (1013 hPa).\n2. Measure the residual offsets recorded on diagnostic registers.\n3. Compare the measured residuals against the limit (\u2264 120 hPa).\n\nIf the residual deviation is beyond the acceptable range, corrective action should be taken according to the specified protocol."
    },
    {
