        "generation": 23679.36,
        "total": 35865.96
      },
      "answer": "The amber warning beacon triggers when the buffered sample count exceeds 80 percent of the FRAM capacity. \n\nFrom Section 3. Telemetry Intervals and Contradictory Maintenance Notes in Source 3, we find:\n\"The primary barometric transducer has a factory accuracy tolerance of plus or minus 0.4 hectopascals.\"\nAnd from Appendix Safety Rule in Source Source 4:\n\"Ambiguous Operational Alert Policy: An amber warning indicator activates when the buffer sample count surpasses 8% of its maximum capacity.\"\n\nThus, based on the given information, the amber warning alert is triggered when the sampled data volume reaches 8%. Given the FRAMS can store up to 14,400 samples, an 8%-threshold would mean triggering occurs when the sample count reaches \\( \\frac{8}{100} \\times 10^4 = 8,000 \\) samples out of 1,440,0 which aligns with the described behavior."
    },
